# The one direction each fusion's composed figure CAN see, and how much of that direction is
# known. One row per fusion that has at least one resolved part: its parts, the dimension of its
# block of the row space, the dimension of its block of the null space, and the state of the
# factors the direction is built out of.
#
# The other half of the part side
#   `F Phi` sends the part space to the fusion space, and the part space splits in two. One
#   fusion's row of `F Phi` is `phi` restricted to that fusion's parts and zero everywhere else,
#   so the rows have disjoint supports and both halves are block diagonal over the SAME fibres:
#   inside one fusion's block, `span(phi)` is the direction the composed figure reads and
#   `{v : phi . v = 0}` is everything it cannot. One plus `parts - 1` is `parts`, per fusion and
#   therefore over the corpus, which is the direct sum written as a count.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `fold_subspaces`.
#
# The dimension is a literal, and the literal is a claim
#   `row_dim` is 1 because `phi` restricted to a non-empty fibre is a non-zero vector, and two
#   things make that true rather than obvious. `pm.part`'s CHECK refuses a factor that is not
#   strictly positive, and an omitted factor is ONE exactly rather than unknown, so no part can
#   contribute a zero. A fusion with no part at all would have no non-zero row and no block, and
#   `algebra/fusions_have_parts.sqlc` is what holds that none is in that state. Written as a
#   column rather than left to a reader, the claim is somewhere a probe can move it.
#
# A positive generator, which is why supply cannot hide in the null space
#   Every entry of the generator is strictly positive, so the row space meets the non-negative
#   orthant. The null space does not: `phi . v = 0` with `phi` positive forces any non-zero `v` to
#   carry a plus and a minus. So the offsets a fusion declared immaterial take from one part
#   exactly what they give another, and there is no non-negative assignment to the parts that the
#   composed figure fails to see. That is the conserved carrier written as a subspace, and it is
#   the half of the picture a dimension alone does not carry.
#
# The direction is known only where the factors are
#   `rank/composition_kernel.sqlc` says why the dimension does not depend on the factors and the
#   direction does. This relation is where that sentence becomes a column: a part whose factor is
#   a typed absence or a derivation is still a part, so the block is still there and still has
#   these two dimensions, and nothing in the corpus says which way either one points. Read
#   `factors_unknown` before believing a basis.
#
# What an indeterminate direction is NOT
#   It is not an unsettled sum. `composition/unsized_conversions.sqlc` is the relation that says
#   the arithmetic cannot be done, and a fusion may sit in one of these states and not the other:
#   a factor filed as a derivation nobody has computed leaves the direction unknown while the
#   figure is still owed. The two questions are about the operand and about the operator.
#
# Resolved parts, because those are the matrix's columns
#   The same set `rank/composition_kernel.sqlc` folds, and for the same reason. The two relations
#   report the same `parts` on every row by construction, which is what lets one law compare a
#   dimension against a rank without either side choosing its own population.
#
# Probe
#   `algebra/composition_row_space.sqlc` holds `row_dim + kernel_dim = parts` per fusion and the
#   sum of `row_dim` against the fusion rows counted in `rank/composition_image.sqlc`. Read
#   `row_dim` as two and both halves fail at once, the first on every fusion and the second with a
#   rank twice the fusion count.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part folded onto its fusion: the fibre's generator, and what it is known to.
SELECT p.composition,
       p.composed_layer,
       count(*)                                                             AS parts,
       1::bigint                                                            AS row_dim,
       count(*) - 1                                                         AS kernel_dim,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE p.factor_state = 'stated')                    AS factors_stated,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE p.factor_state = 'omitted')                   AS factors_one,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation'))   AS factors_unknown,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation')) = 0
                                                                            AS direction_known
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
GROUP BY p.composition, p.composed_layer
